{
  "rules": [
    "When recent history shows a strong upward trend (e.g., >60€) followed by a model forecast that assumes smooth continuation, apply a mean reversion correction of -5% to -8% if sentiment is neutral or slightly negative.",
    "If the true outcome exhibits high volatility with sharp spikes (e.g., sudden jumps from 70€ to 76€) while the model predicts a flat line, reduce the forecast by 3-4 points immediately after a spike occurs to account for mean reversion.",
    "When sentiment history shows a recent positive shift (sentiment > 0.5) but the price action remains volatile or declines, do not increase the forecast; instead, maintain the baseline or apply a slight downward bias (-2%) as market momentum often lags behind sentiment.",
    "In periods of high volatility where the model's standard deviation is underestimated, reduce the forecasted range width by 15% to prevent overconfidence in smooth paths during turbulent markets.",
    "If the last 3 days show a decline despite positive sentiment history, apply an immediate downward correction of -2€ to -4€ as sentiment often fails to translate into price gains quickly."
  ]
}
